During hibernation, create_image() freezes all devices, takes a memory
snapshot, and only then thaws them so swsusp_write() can write the image to
the swap file. The SD host has to stay powered, clocked, and inited
across that whole time period.

The original SET_SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS mapping implements .freeze as
pm_runtime_force_suspend, which powers the MicroSD controller off before
swsusp_write() can trigger any block I/O, and combines it with
pm_runtime_force_resume, which deliberately skips ->runtime_resume for a
device that runtime PM does not expect to be used again. The controller is
therefore left dead at the moment the hibernation image has to be
written through it.

.freeze now leaves the controller running, while .thaw/.restore re-initialise
it through the normal runtime resume path. Re-initialisation is needed
because the SDMMC power domain can be powered off in the noirq stage
(genpd_sync_power_off() does not honour the per-device rpm_always_on flag)
while runtime PM still reports the device as active, so the PM core state
alone cannot be trusted. Runtime PM is first put back in sync with the
hardware using public API only - no PM core internals are messed with - and
pm_runtime_resume_and_get() then runs ->runtime_resume to restore clocks, DMA,
and bus setup.

.suspend/.resume keep using pm_runtime_force_suspend/resume (S3 unchanged),
and .poweroff still powers the controller down for the "platform"
hibernation mode, where the system is powered off right afterwards.
---
 drivers/mmc/host/dw_mmc-rockchip.c |   60 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 58 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/mmc/host/dw_mmc-rockchip.c b/drivers/mmc/host/dw_mmc-rockchip.c
--- a/drivers/mmc/host/dw_mmc-rockchip.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/host/dw_mmc-rockchip.c
@@ -567,9 +567,65 @@ static void dw_mci_rockchip_remove(struc
 	dw_mci_pltfm_remove(pdev);
 }
 
+static int dw_mci_rockchip_reinit(struct device *dev)
+{
+	int ret;
+
+	/*
+	 * The SDMMC power domain can be powered off in the noirq stage of a
+	 * system-wide transition, losing the controller state while runtime PM
+	 * still considers the device active. Put runtime PM back in sync with
+	 * the hardware and let the normal runtime resume path re-initialise the
+	 * controller.
+	 */
+	pm_runtime_disable(dev);
+	pm_runtime_set_suspended(dev);
+	pm_runtime_enable(dev);
+
+	ret = pm_runtime_resume_and_get(dev);
+	if (ret < 0)
+		return ret;
+
+	pm_runtime_put_noidle(dev);
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static int dw_mci_rockchip_freeze(struct device *dev)
+{
+	int ret;
+
+	/*
+	 * swsusp_write() writes the hibernation image through this controller,
+	 * so it must stay powered, clocked, and inited. A non-zero return
+	 * from a system sleep callback is treated as a failure, but
+	 * pm_runtime_resume() returns 1 when the device is already active.
+	 */
+	ret = pm_runtime_resume(dev);
+	if (ret < 0)
+		return ret;
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static int dw_mci_rockchip_thaw(struct device *dev)
+{
+	/* Re-initialise if the power domain was turned off in the noirq stage. */
+	return dw_mci_rockchip_reinit(dev);
+}
+
+static int dw_mci_rockchip_restore(struct device *dev)
+{
+	/* Re-initialise if the power domain was turned off in the noirq stage. */
+	return dw_mci_rockchip_reinit(dev);
+}
+
 static const struct dev_pm_ops dw_mci_rockchip_dev_pm_ops = {
-	SET_SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS(pm_runtime_force_suspend,
-				pm_runtime_force_resume)
+	.suspend = pm_runtime_force_suspend,
+	.resume = pm_runtime_force_resume,
+	.freeze = dw_mci_rockchip_freeze,
+	.thaw = dw_mci_rockchip_thaw,
+	.poweroff = pm_runtime_force_suspend,
+	.restore = dw_mci_rockchip_restore,
 	SET_RUNTIME_PM_OPS(dw_mci_runtime_suspend,
 			   dw_mci_runtime_resume,
 			   NULL)
